<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Lactate Fitness Report</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --surface: #fcfcfb; --card: #ffffff; --border: #e4e3df;
    --text: #0b0b0b; --text-2: #52514e; --text-3: #7a7974;
    --blue: #2a78d6; --orange: #eb6834; --warn: #a15c00;
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body { font-family: -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Arial, sans-serif; margin: 0; background: var(--surface); color: var(--text); line-height: 1.5; }
  main { max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 16px 4rem; }
  h1 { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; font-size: 1.7rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .meta { color: var(--text-3); font-size: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  section { margin-bottom: 2.75rem; }
  .note { color: var(--text-2); font-size: 0.9rem; max-width: 72ch; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .card { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem; }
  .headline { font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .tiles { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(210px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
  .tile .label { color: var(--text-2); font-size: 0.8rem; }
  .tile .value { font-size: 1.45rem; font-weight: 650; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .tile .sub { color: var(--text-3); font-size: 0.8rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .swatch { display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; margin-right: 6px; vertical-align: baseline; }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 0.88rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.45rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: var(--text-2); font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.8rem; }
  td.num, th.num { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }
  .muted { color: var(--text-3); }
  .warn { color: var(--warn); }
  .pill { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.75rem; padding: 0.05rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 999px; border: 1px solid var(--border); color: var(--text-2); white-space: nowrap; }
  ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; } li { margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  details summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--text-2); }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>Lactate Fitness Report</h1>
  <div class="meta">
    Generated {{ summary.generated_at }} · {{ summary.data_summary.n_rows_modeled }} of {{ summary.data_summary.n_rows_total }} reps modeled ·
    {{ summary.data_summary.n_sessions }} sessions · {{ summary.data_summary.date_range[0] }} to {{ summary.data_summary.date_range[1] }}
  </div>

  {% set status = summary.status %}
  <section class="card">
    <div class="headline">{{ summary.headline }}</div>
    <div class="tiles">
      {% for target, value in status.current.items() %}
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label"><span class="swatch" style="background: {{ 'var(--blue)' if target == '2.0' else 'var(--orange)' }}"></span>Pace at {{ target }} mmol/L, standard conditions</div>
        <div class="value">{{ value.median | pace_short }}</div>
        <div class="sub">{{ value.median | pace }} · 95%: {{ value.lo95 | pace_short }}–{{ value.hi95 | pace_short }}</div>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
      {% for c in status.comparisons %}
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Change vs. {{ c.label }}</div>
        <div class="value">{{ c.median | signed_s }}</div>
        <div class="sub">{{ c.verdict }} · {{ (c.prob_faster * 100) | round | int }}% chance you're faster</div>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin: 1rem 0 0;">
      As of the week of {{ status.as_of_week }}. Negative = faster. "Standard conditions" means: track,
      15°C or cooler, first rep of a 5-minute rep with 60 s standing rest, first session of the day.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Is your fitness improving?</h2>
    <p class="note">
      The lines show the pace that would produce exactly 2.0 and 3.0 mmol/L under standard conditions, week by week
      (shaded: 80% and 95% certainty). Each dot is one session, converted to that same standard: its raw pace and
      lactate, corrected for conditions, then shifted along your own lactate curve to the nearest target. Dots
      scatter around the line because of day-to-day form (sleep, fatigue, measurement noise) – about
      ±{{ summary.fitness.day_to_day_form_sd_s_per_km }} s/km for you. The trend is what's left once that noise is averaged out.
    </p>
    {{ figs.fitness_trend | safe }}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Training cycles</h2>
    <p class="note">Average standardized pace at {{ summary.fitness.reference_lactate }} mmol/L over each cycle's weeks with data.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <tr><th>Cycle</th><th>Dates</th><th class="num">Sessions</th><th class="num">Pace at {{ summary.fitness.reference_lactate }} mmol/L</th><th class="num">95% range</th><th>vs. previous cycle</th></tr>
      {% for c in summary.cycles %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ c.label }}</td>
        <td class="muted">{{ c.start_date }} – {{ c.end_date or "ongoing" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ c.n_sessions }}</td>
        {% if c.pace_at_reference %}
        <td class="num">{{ c.pace_at_reference.median | pace }}</td>
        <td class="num muted">{{ c.pace_at_reference.lo95 | pace_short }}–{{ c.pace_at_reference.hi95 | pace_short }}</td>
        <td>{% if c.change_vs_previous %}{{ c.change_vs_previous.median | signed_s }} <span class="pill">{{ c.change_vs_previous.verdict }}, {{ (c.change_vs_previous.prob_faster * 100) | round | int }}% faster</span>{% else %}<span class="muted">–</span>{% endif %}</td>
        {% else %}
        <td class="num muted" colspan="3">no lactate data in this cycle</td>
        {% endif %}
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What makes a session harder or easier</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Every condition is expressed as a pace cost: "+5 s/km" means the condition makes a rep feel like running
      5 s/km faster. Hollow gray markers are what exercise physiology research would expect before seeing your data;
      blue is the estimate after combining that with your data. Where the blue bar is much narrower than the gray one,
      your data taught the model something; where they overlap almost fully, there isn't enough data on that factor
      yet and the number is mostly the literature value.
    </p>
    {{ figs.factors | safe }}
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <tr><th>Factor</th><th class="num">Effect</th><th class="num">95% range</th><th class="num">Reps affected</th><th>Source</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
      {% for f in summary.fitness.factors %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ f.label }}<br><span class="muted">{{ f.unit }}</span></td>
        <td class="num">{{ f.median | signed_s }}</td>
        <td class="num muted">{{ "%+.1f" | format(f.ci95[0]) }} to {{ "%+.1f" | format(f.ci95[1]) }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ f.n_reps_affected }}</td>
        <td>{% if f.learned_from_data %}<span class="pill">learned from your data</span>{% else %}<span class="pill">mostly literature</span>{% endif %}</td>
        <td class="muted">{{ f.rationale }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Your lactate curve</h2>
    {% set curve = summary.fitness.curve %}
    <p class="note">
      Your lactate rises about <strong>{{ curve.slope_pct_per_s_per_km.median }}% per s/km</strong> faster
      (95%: {{ curve.slope_pct_per_s_per_km.lo95 }}–{{ curve.slope_pct_per_s_per_km.hi95 }}%). Going from 2.0 to 3.0 mmol/L
      takes about <strong>{{ curve.s_per_km_for_1_5x_lactate.median }} s/km</strong> more speed
      (95%: {{ curve.s_per_km_for_1_5x_lactate.lo95 }}–{{ curve.s_per_km_for_1_5x_lactate.hi95 }}).
      This is why a simple lactate ÷ speed ratio misleads: running 5 s/km slower lowers lactate by roughly
      {{ (100 * (1 - (2.718281828 ** (-0.05 * curve.slope_pct_per_s_per_km.median)))) | round | int }}% with no change in fitness at all.
      The normalized pace in this report corrects for that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Sessions</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Most recent first. "Lactate ÷ speed" is the metric from the spreadsheet, for comparison. "Normalized" is the
      session converted to 2.0 mmol/L under standard conditions. "Day form" is how far the session sat from your
      trend that week (positive = a better day than expected).
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <tr><th>Date</th><th>Workout</th><th class="num">Reps</th><th class="num">Avg pace</th><th class="num">Avg lactate</th><th class="num">Lactate ÷ speed</th><th class="num">Normalized @ 2.0</th><th class="num">Day form</th></tr>
      {% for s in sessions %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ s.date }} <span class="muted">{{ s.period or "" }}</span></td>
        <td>{{ s.workout_type or "–" }}<br><span class="muted">{{ s.surface or "" }}</span></td>
        <td class="num">{{ s.n_reps }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ s.mean_pace_s_per_km | pace_short }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.1f" | format(s.mean_lactate) }}</td>
        <td class="num muted">{{ "%.2f" | format(s.mean_lactate / (1000 / s.mean_pace_s_per_km)) }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ s.normalized_pace["2.0"] | pace_short }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ s.form_s_per_km | signed_s }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Data quality &amp; caveats</h2>
    {% set dq = summary.data_quality %}
    {% set diag = summary.fitness.diagnostics %}
    <ul>
      <li>
        Model fit: {{ diag.n_reps }} reps in {{ diag.n_sessions }} sessions.
        {% if diag.converged %}Sampling converged (R-hat {{ diag.max_rhat }}, no divergences).
        {% else %}<span class="warn">Sampling did not fully converge (R-hat {{ diag.max_rhat }}, {{ diag.n_divergences }} divergences) – treat numbers with caution.</span>{% endif %}
      </li>
      <li>Temperature was missing for {{ dq.temperature_imputed }} reps and filled in: treadmill as indoor temperature, outdoor with the average of your recorded temperatures in the same month.</li>
      {% if dq.max_effort_rows_excluded %}
      <li>{{ dq.max_effort_rows_excluded }} rep(s) logged as {{ dq.max_effort_workout_types | join(", ") }} were left out: an all-out effort is a different physiological situation from threshold training.</li>
      {% endif %}
      <li>Heart rate is intentionally not used as a factor: it's a result of intensity, not a condition, and correcting for it would remove part of the fitness signal itself.</li>
      <li>One shared lactate-curve slope is assumed over the whole period. If the shape of your curve changes with fitness (not just its position), this simplification hides that.</li>
      <li>A large treadmill effect can also come from belt-speed calibration: if the treadmill's displayed speed is off, the model absorbs that into the treadmill factor.</li>
    </ul>
    <details>
      <summary>How complete each column is</summary>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <tr><th>Column</th><th class="num">% of reps with a value</th></tr>
        {% for col, rate in dq.fill_rates.items() %}
        <tr><td>{{ col }}</td><td class="num">{{ (rate * 100) | round(1) }}%</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </table></div>
    </details>
  </section>
</main>
</body>
</html>
